```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <stdexcept>

class Conn {
    int fd_;
public:
    explicit Conn(int fd) : fd_(fd) {
        if (fd_ < 0) throw std::runtime_error("connect failed");
    }
    ~Conn() { std::cout << "closed\n"; }   // ::close(fd_) in real code
    Conn(const Conn&) = delete;
    Conn& operator=(const Conn&) = delete;
    void send(const char* msg) { std::cout << "send " << msg << '\n'; }
};

int main() {
    try {
        Conn c(7);                          // pretend fd 7 is valid
        c.send("NEW ORDER");
        throw std::runtime_error("mid-session failure");
    } catch (const std::exception& e) {
        std::cout << "recovered: " << e.what() << '\n';
    }
}
```

Output:

```
send NEW ORDER
closed
recovered: mid-session failure
```

The destructor runs during stack unwinding, *before* the catch block executes - so the connection is closed even though no cleanup code was written anywhere near the throw.

independentExercise.prompt:
Two versions of a bracket-order handler take a `bool partial_failure` that throws `std::runtime_error` after acquiring the first leg.

Version A:
```cpp
void apply_A(bool partial_failure) {
    Leg* leg1 = new Leg(1);
    if (partial_failure) throw std::runtime_error("partial");
    Leg* leg2 = new Leg(2);
    delete leg1; delete leg2;
}
```
Version B uses `auto leg1 = std::make_unique<Leg>(1);` and `auto leg2 = std::make_unique<Leg>(2);` in the same positions.

(a) State exactly what is leaked in version A when partial_failure is true, and which of leg1/leg2 leak if the throw is moved *after* the second allocation. (b) State what runs in version B in both cases. (c) Write the one-sentence RAII rule that fixes version A.

